  <!-- Hero (dark) -->
  <section class="hero hero-single">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <p class="eyebrow" data-i18n="proctoring.eyebrow">Ark · Proctoring</p>
        <h1 data-i18n="proctoring.hero_title">The proctor is code on your computer. Not a stranger in a cloud.</h1>
        <p class="lead" data-i18n="proctoring.hero_lead">
          Traditional remote proctoring sends your face, your screen, and your room to a foreign server, where an
          unknown algorithm — or an unknown human — decides whether you cheated. The Attestto Ark Station inverts
          the model: every byte of your exam stays on your machine, and the algorithm that judges you is open source.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a href="#how" class="btn btn-primary" data-i18n="proctoring.hero_cta_primary">How it works</a>
          <a href="/ark/desktop" class="btn btn-secondary" data-i18n="proctoring.hero_cta_secondary">&larr; Station modules</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Why this matters -->
  <section class="section-light">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label" data-i18n="proctoring.problem_label">The problem</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" data-i18n="proctoring.problem_title">Cloud proctoring shouldn't be the only option</h2>
      <p class="section-desc" data-i18n="proctoring.problem_desc1">
        Examity, ProctorU, Honorlock, Respondus — every major remote proctoring vendor follows the same pattern: install a
        browser extension, hand over your camera and microphone, and let a stranger or a third-party AI watch your exam
        in real time on a server you don't control. The video is processed in foreign jurisdictions, retained for
        years, and occasionally leaked. For high-stakes exams in countries with strong data-sovereignty rules, that
        model is structurally incompatible with the law and with basic dignity.
      </p>
      <p class="section-desc" data-i18n="proctoring.problem_desc2">
        The Ark proctoring module is the alternative: a <strong>white-box, local-only</strong> proctor that gives the
        institution forensic-grade exam integrity without ever uploading the candidate's video, screen, or biometric
        data. The institution gets a signed, hashed, tamper-evident exam record. The candidate keeps their privacy.
        The auditor gets source code.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- How it works -->
  <section id="how" class="section-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label" data-i18n="proctoring.how_label">How it works</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" data-i18n="proctoring.how_title">Three layers of local integrity</h2>
      <p class="section-desc" data-i18n="proctoring.how_desc">
        The proctoring module runs as a sub-system of the Attestto Ark Station — the same Electron desktop that holds
        your vault and signs your credentials. Because the Station has Node.js underneath the Chromium engine, it can
        do things a normal browser cannot: directly read camera frames, monitor the desktop, inspect running processes,
        and write a tamper-evident log to disk.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-3">

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">&#127909;</div>
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.layer1_title">1. Direct hardware access</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.layer1_desc">
            The Station uses standard Web APIs to open the camera, but processes the video <strong>locally</strong> on
            the device's CPU/GPU to detect faces and verify liveness. The original frames never leave the machine.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer1_l1">Camera + face detection on-device</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer1_l2">Liveness via blink + head-pose</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer1_l3">Screen monitoring via Electron <code>desktopCapturer</code></li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer1_l4">Process inspection via Node.js</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">&#128737;</div>
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.layer2_title">2. White box, not black box</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.layer2_desc">
            Traditional proctoring (Examity, ProctorU) sends your video to foreign servers for AI analysis. In the Ark,
            the original video <strong>never leaves your device</strong> — the "AI" runs inside the Electron shell on
            your machine, and the algorithm is open source.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer2_l1">No cloud video upload, ever</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer2_l2">No foreign-jurisdiction storage</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer2_l3">Open-source detection models</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer2_l4">Auditable by any university or regulator</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-icon">&#128274;</div>
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.layer3_title">3. Forensic integrity layer</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.layer3_desc">
            Once the exam ends, the Station creates a <strong>SHA-256 hash</strong> of the entire session — anomaly logs,
            timestamps, screen-state digests, biometric confidence scores — and signs it with the candidate's
            <strong>Ed25519</strong> vault key. The institution receives a signed, byte-frozen receipt.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer3_l1">SHA-256 session hash</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer3_l2">Ed25519 signature from vault</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer3_l3">Tamper-evident anomaly log</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.layer3_l4">Verifiable on verify.attestto.com</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- White box vs black box -->
  <section class="section-light">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label" data-i18n="proctoring.compare_label">White box vs black box</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" data-i18n="proctoring.compare_title">Two execution models</h2>
      <p class="section-desc" data-i18n="proctoring.compare_desc">
        The decisive difference is not <em>what</em> the proctor watches, it is <em>where</em> the watching happens
        and <em>who</em> can audit the watcher.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.compare_cloud_title">&#9940; &nbsp;Cloud proctoring</h3>
          <ul>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_cloud_l1">Video uploaded to vendor's server</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_cloud_l2">Stored in foreign jurisdiction</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_cloud_l3">Reviewed by unknown humans or proprietary AI</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_cloud_l4">No source-code access for institutions</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_cloud_l5">Institution has no forensic guarantee — only the vendor's word</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.compare_ark_title">&#9989; &nbsp;Ark white-box proctoring</h3>
          <ul>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_ark_l1">Video processed on the candidate's device</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_ark_l2">Original frames never leave the machine</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_ark_l3">Detection models are open source and auditable</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_ark_l4">Institution receives a signed, hashed exam receipt</li>
            <li data-i18n="proctoring.compare_ark_l5">Independent auditor can verify the entire pipeline</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Technical summary -->
  <section class="section-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label" data-i18n="proctoring.stack_label">Stack</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" data-i18n="proctoring.stack_title">Technical summary</h2>
      <p class="section-desc" data-i18n="proctoring.stack_desc">
        The proctoring module is part of the Attestto Ark Station — the same Electron desktop that holds your vault
        and signs your documents. There is no separate "proctoring app" to install. Every module shares one vault,
        one identity, and one auditable codebase.
      </p>

      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.stack_engine_title">Engine</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.stack_engine_desc"><strong>Electron + Vue 3.</strong> Chromium for the UI, Node.js for hardware. Cross-platform desktop binary.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.stack_detection_title">Detection</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.stack_detection_desc"><strong>Local face + screen monitoring.</strong> Camera frames and screen captures stay on the device. Zero cloud video.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.stack_vault_title">Vault security</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.stack_vault_desc"><strong>xsalsa20-poly1305 + scrypt.</strong> Encrypted local key store. Vault key is the proctoring receipt signer.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.stack_receipt_title">Receipt signing</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.stack_receipt_desc"><strong>Ed25519 over SHA-256 session hash.</strong> Tamper-evident exam record verifiable on any Attestto verifier.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.stack_governance_title">Governance</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.stack_governance_desc"><strong>3-of-4 multisig (planned).</strong> Trust framework keys are split across institutional shareholders. No single party can rewrite records.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.stack_license_title">License</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.stack_license_desc"><strong>Open source.</strong> Apache 2.0. Read the source. Build it yourself. Verify the binary matches.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The verdict -->
  <section class="section-light">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label" data-i18n="proctoring.verdict_label">The verdict</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" data-i18n="proctoring.verdict_title">An auditable proctor that doesn't send your video to the cloud</h2>
      <p class="section-desc" data-i18n="proctoring.verdict_desc1">
        Electron is the perfect engine for the Ark because it lets the proctor be a piece of <strong>auditable code on
        your computer</strong> rather than a stranger in a cloud server. That makes high-stakes remote exams
        technically sovereign and ethically sound — and gives institutions a forensic guarantee that no SaaS vendor
        can credibly match.
      </p>
      <p class="section-desc" data-i18n="proctoring.verdict_desc2">
        If you run a Colegio, a university, a certifying body, or a continuing-education program and you want to offer
        remote exams without giving away your candidates' privacy, the Ark proctoring module is built for you. We are
        opening early-access pilots to a small number of institutions in 2026.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="section-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-label" data-i18n="proctoring.faq_label">FAQ</div>
      <h2 class="section-title" data-i18n="proctoring.faq_title">Hard questions, honest answers</h2>

      <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.faq_q1">Can the Station spy on me beyond the exam?</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.faq_a1">
            No. The proctoring module only activates inside an exam session that the candidate explicitly starts,
            and stops the moment the session ends. Because the source is open, any auditor can verify that the
            camera and screen capture pipelines are not running outside an active exam.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.faq_q2">What if my computer crashes mid-exam?</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.faq_a2">
            The session log is written incrementally to disk and signed at every checkpoint. A crash produces a
            partial signed record up to the last checkpoint. The institution decides whether to allow a re-take based
            on the partial record — there is no proctoring vendor to blame.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.faq_q3">Where is the exam video stored?</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.faq_a3">
            Nowhere outside your machine. The Ark never uploads raw video. What the institution receives is a
            cryptographically signed log of <em>events</em> (face detected, screen anomaly, process opened),
            never the underlying frames.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.faq_q4">Who issues my Ed25519 signing key?</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.faq_a4">
            You do. The Station generates the keypair locally on first launch, stores it in the encrypted vault,
            and never transmits it to Attestto or anyone else. The corresponding public key is what verifiers use
            to confirm a session record came from your machine.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.faq_q5">How does the institution trust the receipt?</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.faq_a5">
            Each receipt embeds the candidate's public key, the SHA-256 of the session log, the timestamps, and the
            Ed25519 signature. The institution verifies the signature against the candidate's enrolled public key —
            the same way verify.attestto.com verifies signed PDFs.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3 data-i18n="proctoring.faq_q6">Can I read the proctoring code?</h3>
          <p data-i18n="proctoring.faq_a6">
            Yes — every line. The Station, the verifier, and the wallet adapter are public on GitHub under
            Apache 2.0. If anything looks wrong, file an issue or open a coordinated disclosure to
            <a href="mailto:security@attestto.com" style="color: var(--color-accent, #22d3a8); font-weight: 600;">security@attestto.com</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

